+# OpenFront on Cloudflare (Workers + Durable Objects + D1)
+
+A port of the game relay in `src/server`, plus the web client, to Cloudflare's Free
+plan. The Node server and every existing package are untouched; this folder is
+self-contained.
+
+The simulation runs in every player's browser, so the server only orders intents
+into numbered turns and hands them to everyone. That small job is what is ported.
+The browser client needs **no source changes**: the Worker speaks the same zbin wire
+protocol (it imports `@openfront/shared` directly) and serves the client the repo
+already builds.
+
+## Layout
+
+| File                    | What it does                                                                                                                                |
+| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `src/index.ts`          | Worker entry. Routes `/w<n>/...` and `/api/...` to the Durable Object for that shard; serves `/w<n>/` page requests from the assets.        |
+| `src/GameHost.ts`       | The Durable Object (one per `/w<n>`): HTTP API, game WebSocket, lobby-list WebSocket, timers, alarm.                                        |
+| `src/Game.ts`           | One game: lobby, prestart/start, 10 turns/s, rejoin, kick, pause, winner vote. Reuses the real `authorizeIntent` rules.                     |
+| `src/PublicLobbies.ts`  | Public matchmaking. Lobbies are a pure function of the clock (see below) and are described in the frames the lobby browser already decodes. |
+| `src/Playlist.ts`       | Map/config for a (type, slot): a small stand-in for `MapPlaylist.ts`.                                                                       |
+| `src/seed.ts`           | Seeded hash/PRNG that make lobbies deterministic.                                                                                           |
+| `src/db.ts`             | D1 history of public games. Best-effort: a D1 failure never affects a game.                                                                 |
+| `src/accounts/`         | Sign-in (Google, emailed link): the account API the client calls, sessions and tokens, the check a game applies to a token (see "Accounts"). |
+| `migrations/`           | D1 schema (public lobbies; accounts).                                                                                                       |
+| `scripts/build-site.ts` | Builds the web client into `../static` and renders its `index.html` (see "The web client").                                                 |
+| `scripts/smoke*.ts`     | End-to-end tests against a running Worker: scripted clients (`smoke`, `smoke:public`) and real Chrome (`smoke:browser`).                    |
+
+## Run it locally
+
+No Cloudflare login and no Docker needed; `wrangler dev` runs the real Workers runtime.
+
+```bash
+cd cloudflare
+npm install                       # this folder is separate from the root workspace
+npm run build:site                # builds ../static (the client); needed once, and after client changes
+npx wrangler d1 migrations apply openfront --local
+echo PUBLIC_LOBBY_SECONDS=8 > .dev.vars   # optional: short countdown for testing (git-ignored)
+npm run dev                       # http://localhost:8787  (the real game)
+
+npm run smoke            # private lobby: join, start, turns, intents, rejoin
+npm run smoke:public     # public matchmaking + D1 (needs PUBLIC_LOBBY_SECONDS=8)
+npm run smoke:browser    # real Chrome: home page, click a lobby, play (needs PUBLIC_LOBBY_SECONDS=8)
+npm run typecheck
+```
+
+Only one `wrangler dev` may run at a time. A stale one keeps answering on the same
+port with old code and settings (a restart can leave a duplicate `workerd`; check
+that exactly one process listens on 8787).
+
+## The web client
+
+`npm run build:site` runs the repo's Vite build, then fills in the EJS `index.html`
+once, with the same values `src/server/RenderHtml.ts` injects per request on the Node
+server, and writes `static/commit.txt`. wrangler serves `static/` through the assets
+binding, which is free and unlimited.
+
+- The page is built **per host**: `SITE_HOST=game.example.com npm run build:site`.
+  The host is baked in (the account API address and the one-server map).
+- `commit.txt` is the build id the page carries. The Worker reads it and refuses joins
+  from any other build, because mismatched clients desync the simulation.
+- **Map budget.** The site is 680 MB, 614 MB of it map files (135 maps), and a deploy can
+  only go as fast as your uplink: at ~110 KB/s that is ~1.7 hours, and wrangler's large
+  parallel batches can sit for 20+ minutes without an answer. `MAP_BUDGET_MB=30` ships a
+  first set (the 6 featured maps, then the smallest) and prunes the rest; `static/maps.json`
+  lists what shipped and the Worker's playlist deals only those, so a public lobby never
+  starts on a map that cannot load. The budget cuts a prefix of a fixed order, so raising it
+  later is a superset and wrangler uploads only the new maps. Maps that did not ship still
+  show in the client's own picker (Solo, private lobbies) and fail to load until a later
+  deploy adds them. Unset, every map ships.
+- `GAME_ENV` is `dev` in the page, so the Worker accepts a guest's anonymous persistent id as
+  well as a signed-in player's token (see "Accounts" below).
+- **No ads, no trackers** (`scripts/third-party.ts`). The upstream page loads OpenFront's own
+  ad, analytics and anti-adblock stack (Google Ads/Analytics under openfront.io's ids, the
+  Playwire/RAMP ad network and its ~30 hosts, Cloudflare Web Analytics under their token,
+  CrazyGames, the AdShield anti-adblock tag) and the client injects the Admiral ad-recovery
+  script. The build removes all of it, forces `window.adsEnabled` to `false`, and then
+  *fails* if any script, stylesheet or image would still come from a host other than
+  Cloudflare Turnstile (joins need its token). Reason: it reports your visitors to someone
+  else's accounts, and on a network that filters those hosts every one hangs the page
+  (`html-load.cc` is filtered in Iran: it resolves to `10.10.34.34` and a connect stalls
+  ~21 s).
+- **Most of OpenFront's account API does not exist here.** The client looks for it at
+  `https://api.<site host>`. Stats, cosmetics, friends, clans, the store and matchmaking are
+  not served, and a failure is only as fast as the DNS miss or round trip behind it (11 s on a
+  slow resolver), so the page carries a small guard that answers those calls with a network
+  error at once. Sign-in and the profile *are* served by this Worker (see "Accounts" below), and
+  the guard lets exactly those through. Upstream code is untouched.
+- **Loading screen** (`scripts/loading-screen.ts`): inline, at the very start of `<head>`,
+  so it paints before the stylesheet and does not wait for it (an inline script cannot run
+  while an earlier stylesheet is still loading, so any later position gives a blank page for
+  exactly as long as a slow connection takes). It retries by itself and then shows a message
+  with a Reload button. `npm run smoke:loading` checks it, including a stalled stylesheet.
+  The picture is a patch of hexagons that get claimed outward from the centre (yellow while a
+  cell is being taken, blue once it is yours), a nod to the game and to the hex grid behind its
+  home page. It is plain SVG and CSS generated at build time, so it needs no script to animate
+  and no extra request. A small "Hosted by hatami-mahdi.ir" link sits at the bottom
+  (`CREDIT_URL` in `scripts/loading-screen.ts`). It opens in a new tab, so the game keeps
+  loading in this one, and it stays on screen if loading fails.
+
+- **Service worker for frozen downloads** (`sw/sw.js`, written to `static/sw.js` by the build,
+  registered by a snippet at the start of `<head>`). On the connection this was built for, about
+  one large download in three freezes near 70 KB (no data and no error for minutes). A Solo game
+  start needs three large files at once (the simulation worker and two map layers), so it nearly
+  never got through and sat on "Starting...". The worker takes over `GET /assets/*` and
+  `/_assets/*` (every name there carries a content hash): it answers from its cache, or downloads
+  the file itself, counts six seconds without data as a dead attempt, drops the connection and
+  tries again (up to eight times), and keeps what it got for good. Once the home page is up it
+  fetches the files a game start needs (`warmList` in `scripts/resilient-assets.ts`: the worker,
+  the default map, the atlases, then the rest of the code) in the background, so Solo usually
+  finds them already cached. It never touches pages, the API, WebSockets or range requests.
+  `?nosw` on any address removes it and its cache. `npm run smoke:flaky` reproduces the freeze
+  with a small proxy in front of the Worker (`BASE=http://localhost:8787`) and checks the game
+  start with the worker off (stuck, the bug) and on (all files arrive), from cache, and `?nosw`.
+  `smoke:loading` turns the worker off, since it would retry the failures that test creates on
+  purpose.
+
+## Pictures and the opening animation
+
+`art/` holds four small WebP files (60-80 KB each, re-encoded from the owner's artwork): a wide and
+a tall backdrop for the loading screen and for the home page. `build:site` copies them to
+`static/art/` with a content hash in the name (`scripts/art.ts`), serves them from this site rather
+than the CDN, and wires them in: the loading screen fades its picture in once downloaded (plain
+navy until then), and the home page's `#background-layer` uses the other pair (tall one on a phone
+held upright). They are kept small on purpose: a big file on the owner's network can freeze, and
+nothing waits for them.
+
+When the app starts the loading screen says "Everything is OK" (Persian: "همه چیز اوکی شد"), holds
+for `okMs` (2.4 s), then opens like an iris onto the home page (`leaveMs`, 1.4 s) while the home
+picture settles in (`html.cf-reveal`). `prefers-reduced-motion` gets a plain fade. Both times can be
+shortened in tests through `window.CF_LOADING`.
+
+## Accounts: sign in with Google or an emailed link
+
+Anyone can play as a guest (a random id kept in the browser). Signing in gives a player the
+same identity on every device, so a private lobby they created, or a game they rejoin, follows
+them. It does not give stats, cosmetics, friends, clans or a store: those belong to OpenFront's
+own closed-source API, which this port does not have.
+
+The client's sign-in screen calls an account API at `https://api.<site host>` (the name is
+fixed by the client). The same Worker answers there (`src/accounts/`), on a second hostname:
+
+| Call | What it does |
+|------|--------------|
+| `POST /auth/magic-link` | emails a one-time link (Brevo), 15 minutes, `#token-login?token-login=…` on the site |
+| `GET /auth/login/token` | the page trades that link for a session |
+| `GET /auth/login/google`, `/auth/callback/google` | Google's authorization-code flow |
+| `GET /auth/link/google` | "Link Google account" for someone already signed in |
+| `POST /auth/refresh` | the session cookie in, a one-hour token (JWT, HS256) out |
+| `POST /auth/logout`, `/auth/revoke` | this browser / every browser |
+| `GET /users/@me`, `DELETE /users/@me` | the profile; delete the account at once |
+
+- **A session** is a random secret in an HttpOnly cookie on the API host; only its SHA-256 is
+  stored (D1, `migrations/0002_accounts.sql`). Emailed links are stored the same way.
+- **A token** is what the client sends to games and to `/users/@me`. Its `sub` is the account
+  id, which is the player's persistent id in a game (`src/accounts/token.ts`); `iss` is
+  `https://api.<site host>` and `aud` the site host, because the client rejects anything else.
+  The game server accepts a guest's UUID or such a token, so nothing changes for guests.
+- **`of_signed_in=1`**: a readable cookie on the site host, set at sign-in. The page asks for
+  a token only when it is there (`scripts/third-party.ts`), so a visitor who never signed in
+  makes no call at all, as before accounts existed. It carries no secret.
+- **The same address is one account.** Google (verified address only) and the email link reach
+  the same account when the address matches. An address already tied to a *different* Google
+  identity is refused (`email_exists`) rather than handed over.
+- **Limits** on the email route: 5 links per address and 15 per network per hour, and
+  `MAIL_DAILY_CAP` (250) a day in all, so the form cannot flood an inbox or use up Brevo's
+  free allowance.
+- **Not offered:** Discord and Steam sign-in, and Steam linking. The page hides those buttons
+  (`scripts/accounts-ui.ts`); a stray visit to them returns to the site with a plain error.
+  `ACCOUNTS=0 npm run build:site` builds a guest-only page that never calls the API.
+
+Without `JWT_SECRET` (or without D1) the API answers 503 and everyone is a guest, as before.
+Google needs its two values and the email link needs Brevo's, each set separately.
+
+**Setting it up** (secrets are set with `wrangler secret put`, never written to a file):
+
+1. `npm run db:migrate:remote` (adds the account tables to the `openfront` database).
+2. `JWT_SECRET`: any long random string, e.g.
+   `node -e "process.stdout.write(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))" | npx wrangler secret put JWT_SECRET`
+3. Google: in Google Cloud Console create an OAuth client ("Web application"), with the
+   authorized redirect URI `https://api.<site host>/auth/callback/google`. Then
+   `npx wrangler secret put GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` and `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`. The consent screen
+   needs only the `openid` and `email` scopes (no Google review).
+4. Email: in Brevo create an API key (SMTP & API → API keys) and authenticate the sending
+   domain (Senders, Domains & Dedicated IPs → Domains: Brevo shows a few DNS records to add in
+   Cloudflare). Then `npx wrangler secret put BREVO_API_KEY`, and set `MAIL_FROM` (an address on
+   that domain, e.g. `login@hatami-mahdi.ir`) and optionally `MAIL_FROM_NAME` as `vars` in
+   `wrangler.jsonc`.
+5. Uncomment the two `api.…` entries in `routes` (`wrangler.jsonc`): deploying them creates the
+   second hostname. Then build for the host and deploy as usual (`SITE_HOST=… npm run
+   build:site`, `npm run deploy:staged`). Until then build with `ACCOUNTS=0`, which leaves
+   sign-in out of the page.
+
+**Tests:** `npm run smoke:accounts` starts its own Worker with a fresh database and stand-ins
+for Google and Brevo (`scripts/accounts-harness.ts`) and runs every route, including joining
+a game with a token and the profile parsed with the client's own schema.
+`npm run smoke:accounts:browser` (after `npm run build:site -- --skip-vite`, a page built for
+localhost) drives the client's real sign-in screen in Chrome: the email form, the emailed link,
+the Google button, "Link Google account", and the page's guard.
+
+Locally, `npm run dev` passes `--local-upstream`: with custom-domain routes configured,
+wrangler dev would otherwise show the Worker the first route's hostname instead of the one the
+request was made to, and the API works out its addresses from the host.
+
+## Serving the files from jsDelivr (for networks that cut large downloads)
+
+On the network this was built for, every large download from a Cloudflare-hosted name is cut
+after 65-170 KB (see "What the network does to it" above), while the same files download fine
+from jsDelivr. The site can therefore be built with its files on a CDN: the page, the game API
+and the WebSocket stay on this Worker, and everything under `/assets` and `/_assets` is loaded
+from `CDN_BASE`. The client supports this natively (`BOOTSTRAP_CONFIG.cdnBase`, relative chunk
+references, the engine worker inlined as a same-origin blob), and the service worker covers the
+CDN origin too.
+
+1. Build once: `SITE_HOST=<host> MAP_BUDGET_MB=30 npm run build:site`.
+2. Stage the files in a git repository jsDelivr will serve:
+   `ASSET_REPO_DIR=../../openfront-assets ASSET_REPO_SLUG=<user>/<repo> npm run cdn:repo`
+   (copies `assets/` and `_assets/`, checks jsDelivr's limits of 20 MB per file and 150 MB
+   per package, commits, prints the commit and the `CDN_BASE`).
+3. Push that repository to GitHub (public), then render the page against the commit:
+   `CDN_BASE=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/<user>/<repo>@<commit> SITE_HOST=<host> npm run build:site -- --skip-vite`
+   (`--skip-vite` re-renders from the template copy in `.build/`, no Vite run) and `npx wrangler deploy`.
+   A commit URL is cached by jsDelivr for good, so each publish is a new commit.
+4. `npm run smoke:cdn` rehearses the whole thing locally against a stand-in CDN
+   (`CDN_BASE=http://localhost:8790/gh/test/assets@local SITE_HOST=localhost npm run build:site`,
+   then `npm run dev` and the test): every file from the CDN, none from the site, a Solo start
+   from the CDN, and the service worker rescuing a start while the stand-in freezes files.
+
+The Worker keeps serving the same files itself, so a visitor on a normal network could also be
+pointed back at them by building without `CDN_BASE`.
+
+## Serving the files from a Tigris bucket (the owner's choice)
+
+The owner wants the files where nobody can browse them. A **public Tigris bucket** serves a file
+only to someone with its exact address, and directory listing is off by default, so this is the
+route in use. Tigris is S3-compatible (`https://t3.storage.dev`, region `auto`); public objects
+are served from `https://<bucket>.t3.tigrisbucket.io/<key>`, on a network path that is not
+Cloudflare's. Free tier: 5 GB, 100,000 downloads a month, no bandwidth charge.
+
+1. The bucket is created and switched to public by its owner in the Tigris console (Settings >
+   Public/Private Access; keep "Disable Directory Listing" on). Making a bucket public needs
+   payment verification for organizations created after May 2026.
+2. `TIGRIS_KEY=... TIGRIS_SECRET=... TIGRIS_BUCKET=openfront-files TIGRIS_IP=92.5.184.207 node scripts/tigris-sync.mjs`
+   uploads `static/assets` and `static/_assets` (content type by extension, one-year immutable
+   caching, skips what is already there, retries). `TIGRIS_IP` pins the API host: one of its
+   addresses (144.24.176.132) never answers from the network this was built on. CORS (GET/HEAD
+   from any origin) must be set once on the bucket (`PutBucketCors`).
+3. Build the page for it: `CDN_BASE=https://openfront-files.t3.tigrisbucket.io SITE_HOST=<host> npm run build:site -- --skip-vite`,
+   then `npx wrangler deploy`. Everything in the jsDelivr section above applies the same way
+   (`CDN_BASE` is just an origin here).
+
+## Serving the files from GitHub Pages (the route in use)
+
+Measured from the network this was built on (plain HTTP/2, default windows, four files at
+once): the Worker's own host never completes a big file, jsDelivr completes most rounds but
+sometimes resets the connection after ~20 s, and `github.io` completed every round, only more
+slowly (~4 MB in 10-19 s). Pages also has no 150 MB package cap (limits: 1 GB site, 100 MB
+file) and its address does not change with each publish.
+
+The repository `MahdiHatami-cpu/mahdi-website` already publishes its `main` branch to Pages
+(`https://mahdihatami-cpu.github.io/mahdi-website/`), and a repository publishes only one
+branch, so the game's files go into a subfolder of it:
+
+1. `CDN_BASE=https://mahdihatami-cpu.github.io/mahdi-website/openfront-files SITE_HOST=<host> ACCOUNTS=0 MAP_BUDGET_MB=150 npm run build:site`
+2. `npm run cdn:pages` (`scripts/pages-repo.ts`) makes one commit, on top of `main` as GitHub has
+   it now, that adds `openfront-files/{assets,_assets}` plus an empty `.nojekyll` at the root, and
+   refuses to finish if the commit touches anything else. `.nojekyll` is required: without it
+   Pages runs Jekyll, which drops every folder starting with an underscore, so `_assets/` would
+   404. The commit is prepared in `../../openfront-pages` (a git worktree of the asset clone, so
+   a push sends only what GitHub does not already have).
+3. The owner pushes it (the script prints the command; it needs their GitHub credentials):
+   `git -c http.version=HTTP/1.1 push https://github.com/MahdiHatami-cpu/mahdi-website.git HEAD:refs/heads/main`
+   Pages then publishes in a minute or two; check a file under `.../openfront-files/assets/`.
+4. Because the base URL has no commit in it, the page built in step 1 is already final.
+   `SKIP_DIRS=assets,_assets npm run deploy:staged` deploys the Worker without those two folders
+   (the page, build id, map list and pictures only), so nothing large goes over the uplink.
+
+Files whose names carry a content hash are only ever added, never changed, so a new publish
+can sit beside the old one; deploy the Worker only after Pages serves the new files.
+
+## Public lobbies are a pure function of the clock
+
+Time is cut into slots of `PUBLIC_LOBBY_SECONDS`. For each type (`ffa`, `team`), slot
+`s` has exactly one lobby whose id, map and settings derive from `(type, s)` and which
+starts at the end of its slot. The slot in progress is the head (counting down); the
+next slot's lobby is queued behind it.
+
+This is not an optimisation, it is what makes hibernation safe. A Durable Object holding
+only hibernating lobby-list sockets is evicted within seconds and everything in memory
+goes with it. A queue kept in memory vanished under the browser's feet, so the lobby a
+player clicked no longer existed (the real-browser test caught this: six cold starts in
+one run). Now a fresh instance recomputes the same lobbies, and a join to a lobby it has
+not created yet creates it on demand. Nothing is stored.
+
+## Staying inside the Free plan
+
+Durable Objects get 13,000 GB-s of duration and 100,000 requests a day, D1 gets
+100,000 row writes a day (check the Cloudflare docs for current numbers).
+
+- **One object per shard, not per game.** N concurrent games share it and bill duration once.
+- **Lobby-list sockets hibernate.** A homepage tab left open does not keep the object awake;
+  an alarm at each slot boundary refreshes the list.
+- **The turn timer stops when nobody is playing,** so the object can hibernate even while
+  empty public lobbies sit in the queue.
+- **D1 writes are rare:** 3 per public game that someone entered (opened, started, ended).
+  Empty lobbies and private lobbies are not recorded.
+
+Game sockets are ordinary sockets, so the object stays in memory (and bills) while anyone
+plays. Roughly 28 game-hours a day fit in the free duration allowance.
+
+## What is not ported
+
+Matchmaking works for `ffa` and `team`; the rest of the Node server's surface does not.
+
+- Ranked queues (1v1, 2v2, clan wars), `special` lobbies, hosted/listed lobbies, pools.
+- Everything an account would carry besides a sign-in: cosmetics, friends, stats, clans, the
+  store, bans, Discord/Steam sign-in. The client keeps calling those parts of the closed-source
+  API and the page answers them with an immediate network error, which it tolerates.
+- Desync detection, the replay archive and winner replay, the admin bot, live stats.
+- Rate limiting beyond a per-connection message cap.
+
+State lives in memory, as on the Node server: if the object is evicted or a new version is
+deployed, games in flight are lost (a game with players keeps the object alive).
+
+## Deploying
+
+Needs your Cloudflare account, so it is not done by the tests above.
+
+1. `npx wrangler login`
+2. `npx wrangler d1 create openfront`, then put the printed `database_id` in `wrangler.jsonc`.
+3. `npx wrangler d1 migrations apply openfront --remote`
+4. `SITE_HOST=<your host> MAP_BUDGET_MB=30 npm run build:site` (raise the budget on later deploys)
+5. `npx wrangler deploy`, or `npm run deploy:staged` on a slow uplink (it uploads in small
+   steps, each with a timeout, and skips files wrangler already has; `FROM_STAGE=n` resumes).
+
+To use a custom domain, build with `SITE_HOST=<that domain>` and add it to `routes` in
+`wrangler.jsonc` as a Custom Domain (Cloudflare creates the DNS record and certificate). If
+something else on the zone answers for the name first (a catch-all), also add a route for
+exactly that hostname (`<host>/*` with `zone_name`), which is more specific and wins.
+`workers_dev: true` keeps the `*.workers.dev` address, which adding `routes` would switch off.
+
+**What the network does to it (measured from Iran, Wi-Fi + router).** Large downloads from this site
+freeze for Chrome and Edge, and the cause turned out to have two parts:
+
+1. **Encrypted Client Hello (ECH).** Cloudflare publishes ECH keys in the DNS HTTPS record of every
+   proxied hostname of a zone with ECH on (the default on Free): `openfront.io.hatami-mahdi.ir`,
+   `clipboard.hatami-mahdi.ir` and the account's `workers.dev` names all carry `ech=`, while cdnjs,
+   jsDelivr and developers.cloudflare.com do not. Chrome/Edge then connect with ECH, and on this
+   network such connections die after ~60-500 KB. Same Chrome, same machine, same minutes, a
+   WebSocket download of the same 755 KB / 2 MB files: default (ECH on) 0 of 24, Chrome with
+   `--ssl-version-max=tls1.2` 4/4 in every one of 4 runs, Chrome with the DNS HTTPS lookup disabled
+   (so no ECH) 4/4 in both runs. Node, curl and Firefox-style clients that do not use ECH are
+   unaffected. **Done on 2026-11-03: ECH is off for the zone `hatami-mahdi.ir`** (it was on by default; the DNS HTTPS record no longer carries `ech=`). It was not enough on its own: plain Chrome still loads the site unreliably (item 2). To redo it: **turn ECH off for the zone** (dashboard > the domain > SSL/TLS > Edge
+   Certificates > Encrypted Client Hello (ECH) > Off; API `PATCH /zones/<id>/settings/ech`), then
+   wait for DNS caches (the record's TTL is minutes) and restart the browser.
+2. **HTTP/2 with client data in flight.** An ordinary HTTP/2 client (default 64 KB windows, e.g.
+   Node's `http2`, no ECH involved) freezes at 65,535 to ~170,000 bytes on every name that points at
+   this account (the same happens on the Yuki bot's `workers.dev` pictures), while HTTP/1.1,
+   WebSocket and HTTP/2 with huge windows complete in about a second. What a client sends *while*
+   a download is running (window updates, new requests on the same connection) seems to be lost.
+   This one is not understood and not yet fixed; sequence for a fix: ECH off first, retest, then
+   consider delivering files over WebSockets (a WebSocket download needs nothing sent back and
+   completed 16 of 16 from Node, 0.3-0.5 s each).
+
+Not the cause: DNS answers (intermittently slow, for any name), the hostname (`openfront.io.*`,
+`openfront.*`, `play.*` and `workers.dev` all behave alike), IPv6, address pool, Pages vs Workers,
+how the Worker streams the bytes (buffered, streamed, 4/16 KB chunks, paced: identical freezes),
+the files, or the Worker (local tests are clean). HTTP/3 (QUIC) is blocked outright. Test results
+vary with the time of day, so compare variants back to back, never against an earlier run.
+The service worker above keeps what does arrive.
